Anything can be written "readable" to a long time
user of a particular programming language. Even APL
is easy to read _if_ you understand the dingbats in
which it is programmed, profoundly from long use.

That's why convincing a language zealot of a
particular programming language that his or her
favorite  language has been beaten from birth with
an ugly stick until it is flatter than roadkill, is
so hard a task.

[The above linked example is profoundly not such an
easily understood "anything", it is Perl even its
mother would rather not face before coffee.]

It takes language designs deliberately targeted that
way for reasons having to do with the purpose of the
language, like COBOL 1968 [which dealt with Other
People's Money, and so was _required_, probably by
law, certainly by common sense, to be auditable by a
non-programmer accountant such as a government
regulatory agency staff-person] to be read with
understanding by a non-fan, non-user of the
language. 

Wirth's original 1972 Pascal, designed strictly as a
teaching language, for example, gave preference to
words as delimiters instead of punctuation
characters, and is quite accessible to persons
coming from anywhere in the imperative programming
language community.

C, also of that vintage, came from people who, from
all evidence, typed with pencils held in their
teeth, and begrudged every keystroke, the crowd that
also gave us Unix and decided that "remove" was too
hard to type, so "rm" would suffice, that "creat"
would suffice without the trailing "e", that opaque
"cat", short for the obscure "catenate", short for
the at least well recognized "concatinate", would
save one keystroke over "type" or "join", and that
"BEGIN" "END" were too hard to type, so "{" "}"
would have to do, that large ugly expressions should
be abbreviatable with short ugly macros defined in
really bizarre ways with horribly obscure "gotcha"s,
that expanded invisibly to those large ugly
expressions, hiding really subtle bugs in the
process.

The result is, or can be, some of the ugliest
imaginable code when viewed by a programmer with
broad experience among programming languages.

That, sadly, is the source code language for
NetHack, and there's a reason NetHack source divers
are so few and far between.

